  \uses{lem:ms-one-way-rigidity, lem:qld-ms-anticommutator-original-state, def:ms-prescribed-distances}
  The two ideal Pauli effects act identically on the EPR state. Insert the
  transported original state and apply the squared three-term triangle
  inequality to obtain the agreement transfer. Wrong-form effects have
  original squared action at most $36\eps$; removing them at bit zero costs
  $144\eps+4s^2$ and doubles a completed distance. For products, first move
  the other variable to its opposite-player constraint observable. The
  anticommutator changes by at most $60\sqrt\eps$ on the original state,
  giving prescribed norm at most $1148\sqrt\eps$. Transport to the ideal
  state costs at most $8s$ in norm. The gap note gives the calculations and
  the restricted sense in which the linear agreement term is necessary.
\end{proof}

